# guard-populations.manifest — the coverage boundary of the `--population`
# reverse index (your-org/nexus-code#803), recorded as DATA rather than prose,
# because prose cannot be made to fail.
#
# Enforced by:  bash monitor/watcher/test-guards-for-diff.sh
# Consumed by:  bash monitor/guards-for-diff.sh
# Protocol:     monitor/_guard_population.sh
#
# THE AXIS is HOW A GUARD DECLARES WHAT IT READS — the axis the mechanism
# varies on, not the axis a search varied on. A guard is in this index iff it
# implements the protocol; there are exactly two ways it does so, and column 3
# says which, because they fail differently:
#
#   classifier  the suite forwards to its own generator's `--population`
#               (`bash "$CLASSIFIER" --population`). The declaration IS the
#               enumeration the classifier walks, so it cannot drift from it.
#   inline      the suite declares by calling its own in-file enumerator. Same
#               rule, one level closer: `gp_population` must CALL that function,
#               never restate what it currently returns.
#
# THE CLAIM THIS FILE MAKES IS STRUCTURAL, NOT BEHAVIOURAL. A row does not
# assert that the listed guard would catch any particular defect. It asserts
# the checkable things: this guard declares a population, that population is at
# least `floor` files, every path in it EXISTS, and the set of declaring guards
# is exactly these rows. Existence and not tracked-ness, deliberately: a
# population is computed from the WORKING TREE, so a file written and not yet
# committed is legitimately in it — asserting tracked-ness would red the suite
# for the ordinary act of adding a file. A guard that starts or stops
# declaring lands RED here rather than silently changing what the index covers.
#
# THE FLOOR IS A NON-VACUITY RATCHET, not a size assertion. It is set well
# under the live count so ordinary growth or pruning never trips it, and it
# exists because an enumerator broken into returning NOTHING makes the index
# report "this guard does not read your diff" — indistinguishable, downstream,
# from a correct exclusion. That is this repo's dominant defect class (silence
# read as absence), and it has produced three confident wrong answers here
# already: `mapfile` in zsh, `grep -r` over `reports/`, `git ls-tree` with a
# glob pathspec.
#
# THE FLOOR IS NECESSARY AND NOT LOAD-BEARING, AND THAT IS WHY COLUMN 4 EXISTS
# (your-org/nexus-code#943). A floor says "at least N", which is satisfied by
# any N — so it catches an enumerator that returns NOTHING and nothing else. It
# is silent on the case that actually degrades an index: an enumerator that
# returns LESS. Measured on `e256d4a`, floor versus live population:
#
#     ambient-shell-option-scope  300 / 485      early-exit-reader  300 / 486
#     spawn-shape                 400 / 670      stub-claude        200 / 323
#     summary-honesty             200 / 322      tmux-window-resolver 100 / 151
#
# So a guard could lose 38% of what it reads — 485 files down to 300 — and stay
# green, while `guards-for-diff` quietly told every worker "this guard does not
# read your diff". That is the same sentence a correct exclusion prints.
#
# SENTINELS ARE A MEMBERSHIP ASSERTION, NOT A SIZE ONE. Column 4 names specific
# files the declared population MUST CONTAIN, chosen from structurally
# different corners of each set — the extensionless `monitor/ng` (`#803`'s
# first occurrence, and the house trap a `*.sh` glob misses), a nested
# `test-integration/` path (the clause a filename-shaped guess omits), a
# non-shell member where the population has one. A narrowing that stays above
# the floor still has to keep every one of them, and a narrowing that drops a
# corner is exactly the narrowing a size bound cannot see.
#
# WHY THIS DOES NOT BECOME A GATE THAT ALWAYS FIRES — the failure mode an exact
# size pin would have here. Sentinels are CHECKED-IN DATA that does not move
# when the scan does (`#839`/`#852`'s third-source principle: two enumerators
# reading the same tree agree with each other and confirm nothing). They do not
# grow with the corpus, so adding or pruning ordinary files never trips them;
# only losing a named corner does. The floor STAYS — it is cheap and it catches
# the zero case first, with a clearer message.
#
# ADDING A ROW: pick sentinels from the population you just declared, not from
# the reason you wrote for it, and prefer members whose PATH SHAPE differs from
# the majority. A sentinel that looks like every other member tests the same
# thing the floor already tests.
#
# HOW THIS FILE IS MEANT TO CHANGE. It should only ever GROW. Teaching another
# guard `gp_population` adds a row and shrinks the invisible residual that
# `guards-for-diff.sh` prints on every run. Removing a row means a guard
# stopped declaring, which is a regression in coverage and should be argued for
# in the commit message, not absorbed by regenerating this file.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. The other tracked test suites — the residual —
# are not listed. Listing 291 rows of "does not declare" would be a list of
# what has not been done yet, which rots on every added suite and asserts
# nothing; the residual is COUNTED from the live enumeration instead, printed
# by guards-for-diff.sh and asserted by test-guards-for-diff.sh, so it cannot
# be stale. What is claimed here is the boundary's near side, measured.
#
# Columns (TAB-separated):
#   1 suite      repo-relative path — the thing a worker runs
#   2 floor      minimum population size; refuse below it (the zero-case ratchet)
#   3 kind       classifier | inline
#   4 sentinels  comma-separated members the population MUST contain — the
#                load-bearing half; a narrowing above the floor still has to
#                keep every one
#   5 reason     what this guard reads, and why that is its population
#
monitor/watcher/test-ambient-shell-option-scope.sh	300	inline	monitor/ng,monitor/watcher/test-integration/_harness.sh,monitor/client/nexus-reply-watch	every shell file under monitor/ via _aso_shell_files — P1 and P2 both walk it, and #803's first occurrence was monitor/ng entering P2's population
monitor/watcher/test-claude-md-guards-for-diff.sh	6	inline	monitor/ng,CLAUDE.md,monitor/watcher/test-guards-for-diff.sh,monitor/watcher/_test_helpers.sh	the CLAUDE.md bullet it pins, the index whose exit-code contract it pins, the ng dispatcher the documented spelling goes through, the protocol library its fail-closed fixture sources, and the guard whose own --population supplies its independent hit/miss controls — it declares because its Control D plants a complete protocol fixture, so its bytes carry both discovery tokens while only quoting them
monitor/watcher/test-grep-delegation-arms.sh	4	inline	CLAUDE.md,monitor/grep-delegation-arms.manifest,monitor/grep-delegation-arms.sh	the tool, its manifest and CLAUDE.md — the three files whose disagreement IS the defect (your-org/nexus-code#1234). CLAUDE.md is a sentinel and not decoration: the manifest cites its `<!-- BEGIN … -->` blocks by name and this suite asserts those blocks really exist, so an edit that renames or removes one must select this guard. The tool and manifest are sentinels because a dependency row added to one without the other is exactly the drift nobody would otherwise notice — the arm set is an unversioned external and this trio is the only record of what the documentation assumes about it
monitor/watcher/test-heartbeat-schema-agreement.sh	3	inline	monitor/pane-state.sh,monitor/worker-heartbeat.sh,monitor/hooks/async-launch-detect.sh	the heartbeat SCHEMA CONTRACT between its one production writer, the hook that rewrites its rows, and the classifier that reads it: every field pane-state.sh reads from a heartbeat file must be one the writer emits, derived from both sources rather than restated. A reader of a never-written field is a dead arm that cannot fail, log or go red (your-org/nexus-code#1374). Sentinels are the three files whose edit can change the verdict.
monitor/watcher/test-guards-for-diff.sh	5	inline	monitor/watcher/guard-populations.manifest,monitor/_guard_population.sh,monitor/watcher/test-tmux-window-resolver.sh	the index, the protocol library, this manifest, and the UNION of the guards the manifest NAMES with the guards the TREE says declare (your-org/nexus-code#1170) — it runs each of their probes, so an edit to any of them can change its verdict. The union is what makes the population SELF-COMPLETING: derived from the manifest alone it was the artefact this guard CHECKS, so a suite that BEGINS declaring entered no population, the guard was truthfully excluded, and the one assertion that could catch it was never selected. On a healthy tree the two derivations agree, so the union is byte-identical to the manifest side and costs nothing until it is needed; also the honest resolution of the planted-fixture discovery ambiguity, since no text predicate separates quoting the protocol from implementing it
monitor/watcher/test-early-exit-reader-manifest.sh	300	classifier	monitor/ng,monitor/watcher/test-integration/_harness.sh,monitor/client/nexus-reply-watch	early-exit-readers.sh --population: git-tracked monitor/** filtered by the shared shf_is_shell predicate, BEFORE the pipefail-axis narrowing
monitor/watcher/test-spawn-shape-manifest.sh	400	classifier	monitor/ng,monitor/watcher/test-integration/_harness.sh,CHANGELOG.md	spawn-shapes.sh --population: every tracked file, because the classifier greps them all for new-window — the narrowing to matches is a speed optimisation, not the read set
monitor/watcher/test-stub-claude-manifest.sh	200	classifier	monitor/watcher/test-integration/_harness.sh,monitor/watcher/test-zsh-modifier-lint.sh	stub-claude-fixtures.sh --population: every test-*.sh under monitor/ plus every shell file in a test-integration/ tree (the clause a filename-shaped guess omits)
monitor/watcher/test-summary-honesty-manifest.sh	200	inline	monitor/watcher/test-integration/_harness.sh,monitor/watcher/test-zsh-modifier-lint.sh	the git ls-files '*test-*.sh' corpus it classifies — a suite that prints no banner today is exactly the one that acquires one
monitor/watcher/test-tmux-selection-restore.sh	4	inline	monitor/_tmux-window.sh,monitor/watcher/_respawn.sh,monitor/cc-auto-update-apply.sh	the selection capture/restore helper, the single restore site, the cc-update verb whose call ORDER part C reads from source, and the tmux fixture library the suite sources (your-org/nexus-code#1528). Declared at BIRTH. Floor 4 against a live 4 + self + protocol library: a fixed list, so the floor is the whole list and the sentinels are its three subjects — a narrowing that drops any one of them drops the thing under test
monitor/watcher/test-tmux-window-resolver.sh	100	inline	monitor/assert-bot-author.sh,monitor/pane-state.sh,monitor/write-probe.sh	production shell under monitor/ via _resolver_scan_files — D1 enumerates it twice (by name, by behaviour) and F1 greps it for tmux format strings; #803's third occurrence reddened both
monitor/watcher/test-run-tests-empty-outfile.sh	2	inline	monitor/watcher/run-tests.sh,monitor/watcher/test-run-tests-empty-outfile.sh	run-tests.sh plus this suite's own source — the guard asserts a property of the RUNNER (that an empty out_file base is refused rather than written into $PWD), so an edit to run-tests.sh is exactly what can change its verdict; gp_handle adds the suite itself
monitor/watcher/test-run-tests-accounting-verdict.sh	2	inline	monitor/watcher/run-tests.sh,monitor/watcher/test-run-tests-accounting-verdict.sh	run-tests.sh plus this suite's own source — every assertion is a property of the RUNNER (that a verdict is refused when the record disagrees with what was dispatched, and that the nproc cap is soft-only), so an edit to run-tests.sh is exactly what can change its verdict; gp_handle adds the suite itself
monitor/watcher/test-helper-honesty.sh	300	classifier	monitor/watcher/undefined-helper-lint.sh,monitor/watcher/uhl-unknown-callsites.manifest,monitor/watcher/_shell_quotes.awk,monitor/test-conflict-marker-lint.sh,monitor/watcher/test-integration/test-graceful-exit-relaunch.sh	undefined-helper-lint.sh --population: EVERY tracked test-* suite under monitor/ that shf_is_shell accepts (your-org/nexus-code#1364 — it used to be only the suites that SOURCE _test_helpers.sh, which left 193 of 454 checked by nothing), plus the lint source, the shared quote machine its stripper needs, and the UNKNOWN residue manifest it ratchets against — #1030 F2, where the lint was reachable by no gate but this suite and selectable by none. Sentinels are the two corners the OLD population dropped: a non-sourcing suite outside monitor/watcher/ that defines its own ok/bad (the #922 shape), and a nested test-integration/ path. Floor 300 against a live 457 at 741191c8: a collapse back to the sourcing set (264) trips it
monitor/watcher/test-empty-needle-local-copies.sh	80	inline	monitor/test-ng-cross-surface.sh,monitor/watcher/test-bootstrap-install.sh,monitor/watcher/_test_helpers.sh	every file defining its OWN assert_contains/assert_not_contains, tracked AND untracked, via _enc_population — the guard EXTRACTS and RUNS each definition, so its verdict depends on those bytes; the shared definer is included because #1038 lives there and B asserts it still refuses
monitor/watcher/test-empty-needle-vacuous-helpers.sh	100	inline	monitor/watcher/test-integration/_harness.sh,monitor/test-paste-followup-confirm.sh,monitor/watcher/_test_helpers.sh	every SUITE file (defines an assert_* or sources _test_helpers.sh), tracked AND untracked, via _env_suite_files — the guard EXTRACTS every function definition in them and EXECUTES the verdict-rendering ones, so its verdict depends on those bytes; sentinels are the three corners a filename-shaped guess drops: a nested test-integration/ path, a suite outside monitor/watcher/, and the non-test-named shared definer
