monitor/watcher/test-public-mirror-build-entry-guard.sh	3	inline	monitor/public-mirror/sync-base.sh,monitor/test-public-mirror-scrub.sh,monitor/watcher/test-public-mirror-dictionary-coverage.sh	build.sh and the scrubber it drives (both copied into every throwaway fixture and executed), plus every in-tree file that NAMES build.sh at all — keyed on APPLICABILITY, not on conformance (your-org/nexus-code#1197). It used to enumerate callers whose invocation already CARRIED --yes, which made the population the set of already-compliant callers: dropping --yes at sync-base.sh:104 removed that file from the population and DESELECTED this guard (measured: 6 guards selected before the edit, 5 after, exit 0, this one listed under CONSIDERED AND EXCLUDED). The key is bare rather than path-qualified because sync-base.sh invokes it through a variable, `bash "$REL_PM/build.sh"`, so a path-qualified pattern drops the one production caller — which is this row's first sentinel. --yes is the ASSERTION now, never the filter: §3/§4 pin the exit contract those call sites depend on, so dropping a flag is exactly the edit this guard must be selected by; the sentinels are the caller in monitor/ (not monitor/watcher/) and the one under public-mirror/ itself
